$\begin{align}
\text{vertex's }t\text{-coordinate}&=\dfrac{(\goldD{10})+(\goldD{-1})}{2}
\\\\
&=\blueD{\dfrac{9}{2}}
\end{align}$

Now we can find the vertex's $y$-coordinate by evaluating $g\left(\blueD{\dfrac{9}{2}}\right)$:

$\begin{align}
g\left(\blueD{\dfrac{9}{2}}\right)&=\left(\blueD{\dfrac{9}{2}}-10\right)\left(\blueD{\dfrac{9}{2}}+1\right)
\\\\
&=\left(-\dfrac{11}{2}\right)\left(\dfrac{11}{2}\right)
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{121}{4}
\end{align}$
